In article <1ti3ac$gll@corax.udac.uu.se> hansen@corax.udac.uu.se (Hans
Engstr|m) writes: 
>Here are some misc. questions which may or may not contain some spoilers...

>1. Is there an option to get Nethack to keep your savefile at another
>   directory, instead of the regular save-directory.

	That depends entirely upon the machine on which you're playing.  I
know for certain that the MS-DOS version has such an option, and describes it
in the distributed config file.

>2. How do you zap a wand ( i.e. a wand of digging ) down ? I have tried
>   the down-arrow key but without success.

	To zap a wand at the floor, hit '>' when it asks you what direction to
aim the wand.

>3. What is a horn of plenty ? What happens when you apply it ?

	A horn of plenty is a magic item that creates food.

>4. I beleive that there is a way to refresh your spellbooks, ( besides
>   writing with a magic marker ). If that is so, what is it ?

	Your belief is not grounded in reality.  There is no such way.

>5. What are the effects of a amulet of change ?

	An amulet of change alters your character's gender.

>6. I have seen ascension posts with dex, wis and int greater than 18.
>   My Valkyrie can't seem to get more than 18 ( quaffing blessed potion
>   of Gain Ability ). Why? Is it class-dependant?

	There are certain magical items that will enhance your character's
abilities beyond the maximum attainable by humans.  Specificly, gauntlets of
power/dexterity, helms of brilliance, and rings of adornment do exactly that.

>7. The oilskin cloak is supposed to give rust protection. Is that so also
>   for armor worn under the cloak, ( but not boots, helmet, gloves ... )?

	Any cloak work will protect body armor worn underneath it from acid
attacks and rust traps.  I'd imagine that the virtue of oilskin cloaks protect
body armor from rusting due to immersion as well (should your character find
an amulet of magical breathing and decide to go for a swim.)

>8. When I #sit on thrones they usually vanish in a puff of smoke. Is that
>   normal or is it more to it than that? Should I wait some turns between
>   sitting on them?

	That's not uncommon.  Infrequently they do more than that (magic
mapping, see invisible, genocide, identify some items, blind the character, or
summon a crowd of monsters.)  I don't think it matters at all if you wait a
few turns between #sittings, except as regards your luck.

